WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.860
In the first film, a cell read how long a signal lasted and how fast spikes arrived.

2
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:14.180
When an outside stimulus stays steady, how does a cell make pulses, or keep one

3
00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:19.840
signal local? Both answers come from how cells wire loops and organize space.

4
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:25.613
Negative feedback normally pulls an active pathway back to steady level.

5
00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:28.953
Add a delay, and the brake arrives late.

6
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:34.453
Like a shower tap on a long pipe, you overshoot too hot, then too cold.

7
00:00:35.773 --> 00:00:40.573
When the feedback is strong and its proteins short-lived, activity can rise and fall

8
00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:41.573
in pulses.

9
00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:44.597
Watch NF-κB inside this cell.

10
00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:52.257
A steady signal triggers destruction of its inhibitor, IκB, freeing NF-κB to enter

11
00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:57.407
the nucleus. There, activation builds its own brake.

12
00:00:57.407 --> 00:01:03.137
NF-κB switches on the gene for a fresh inhibitor, IκBα, which takes time.

13
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:09.857
When fresh IκBα arrives, it binds NF-κB and pulls it back out.

14
00:01:11.057 --> 00:01:15.937
Because the steady stimulus stays on, the new inhibitor breaks down too, starting

15
00:01:15.937 --> 00:01:19.657
the next pulse. Now stay in this cell to see place.

16
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.900
One messenger can carry different orders in different spots of a cell.

17
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:31.140
An anchoring protein parks the kinase PKA beside one membrane channel.

18
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.980
A local cloud of cAMP switches on that nearby PKA.

19
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.700
Identical PKA parked across the cell may never see it.

20
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:45.993
Proteins can also build a signaling spot on demand.

21
00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:51.853
On a glass slide with no cell, a kinase put phosphate on a receptor tail.

22
00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:55.933
Phosphates then reached the purified adaptor LAT.

23
00:01:57.393 --> 00:02:02.473
Many weak contacts pulled it and its partners into a liquid-like cluster that sorts

24
00:02:02.473 --> 00:02:03.473
enzymes.

25
00:02:04.217 --> 00:02:09.597
On the slide, that cluster pulls kinases in and keeps phosphatases, the enzymes

26
00:02:09.597 --> 00:02:11.537
removing phosphates, outside.

27
00:02:12.957 --> 00:02:15.077
Predict what happens inside the cluster.

28
00:02:16.397 --> 00:02:21.217
Compared with outside, will phosphate signals inside stay on longer, or shut off

29
00:02:21.217 --> 00:02:22.217
faster?

30
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:32.523
They stay on longer, because adding phosphates speeds up while removing them slows

31
00:02:32.523 --> 00:02:39.183
down. In living cells, the usual test bleaches a bright spot with a laser and times

32
00:02:39.183 --> 00:02:45.843
its refill. Refilling shows molecules exchange, which happens in a droplet and also

33
00:02:45.843 --> 00:02:47.083
on a fixed scaffold.

34
00:02:48.263 --> 00:02:50.403
So cellular droplets remain debated.

35
00:02:51.517 --> 00:02:56.917
Cells encode meaning in feedback loops and in space, not just in molecule names.

36
00:02:58.377 --> 00:03:03.677
Delayed negative feedback turns steady cues into pulses, and location decides which

37
00:03:03.677 --> 00:03:07.617
targets answer. Read on for circuits that test both.
